WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.025 --> 00:00:03.570
[INAUDIBLE] from people who
are chatting in online, and I

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:07.970
wanted to get started just by asking each
of you to briefly introduce yourselves and

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:09.530
talk a little bit about your background.

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:15.251
What were you doing before you applied
to the Stanford MBA program and

00:00:15.251 --> 00:00:19.698
what ultimately led you to
pursue an MBA at Stanford.

00:00:19.698 --> 00:00:20.255
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:00:20.255 --> 00:00:21.718
I can start.

00:00:21.718 --> 00:00:28.010
So my name is Sara I graduated in 2010
studied economics from Chicago originally.

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:31.900
And before the GSB I had tried two things.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:36.970
First I worked in the capital markets on
the banking side at Morgan Stanley and

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:42.360
then moved over to consulting and
was with BCG for three years.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:47.140
The reason I decided to apply to
the business school in general and

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:52.460
also the GSB was I really wanted
to get into impact investing, and

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:57.330
it was something that I didn't really see
a path toward, and also wanted to explore

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:03.935
before I really dedicated myself to it and
so have gotten to do so while here.

00:01:03.935 --> 00:01:05.380
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thanks.

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:07.720
&gt;&gt; And I worked for
a few years in consulting,

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.350
two years in consulting in Atlanta and

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:13.120
then worked for four years in in social
enterprise in Sub-Saharan Africa.

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:16.960
Primarily within operations roles.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.360
What made me most interested
about coming back to GSP and

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:22.868
NVA was I was interested in
moving back to the states.

00:01:22.868 --> 00:01:25.360
And the MBA seemed like a very
good way to move back stateside.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.740
And I was interested in developing
skills kind of beyond core operations

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:29.660
supply chain skills.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:32.330
Specifically within leadership and
general strategy.

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:33.030
&gt;&gt; Awesome.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:34.740
That's really interesting.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:38.350
So we've got a few questions that have
been sent to us already via chat.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:40.600
Somebody's asking us,

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:45.810
what are the most common careers that
Stanford MBAs Pursue after you graduate.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:49.200
And I know this is a really personal
question, so maybe each of you can just

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.410
talk about, what were your career
goals coming into Stanford?

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:55.410
And what are you planning
to do after you graduate?

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:58.200
And what are some of the resources
that you're using at Stanford to help

00:01:58.200 --> 00:01:59.813
you navigate that path?

00:01:59.813 --> 00:02:03.710
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; So coming into Stanford,

00:02:03.710 --> 00:02:08.450
I was very interested in working within
&gt;&gt; Entrepreneurship and a startup.

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:13.310
Probably specifically within
a social enterprise space again.

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.930
Over my time here I've pivoted a little
bit more toward being interested in

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:16.800
healthcare.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:21.700
And upon graduation I'll be working with
a very large medical device company in

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:26.100
a merging market strategy, so
kind of combining two of those together.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:29.120
The resources I used
were on campus recruiting

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:32.810
&gt;&gt; Which happens both in

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:34.330
first year and second year.

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:37.550
And I actually ended up taking a full
time offer with the firm I interned

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:38.610
with this summer.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:43.280
But, before doing that, before accepting
that offer I also considered consulting,

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:44.390
also through on campus recruiting.

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:45.240
And then one or

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:48.574
two other opportunities that came
through with speaking with other alumni.

00:02:48.574 --> 00:02:51.480
That were also working in the
&gt;&gt; In the healthcare space.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:57.460
&gt;&gt; Awesome and I mentioned that
I was interesting on testing

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:59.960
my thesis on impact investing.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:05.510
And so to do that the GSB has a resource
called the impact labs program.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:07.600
And so I got to apply and intern for

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:11.420
an impact investment fund,
which was exactly what I wanted to do.

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:16.130
And, got to work at a fund,
during the year, tested that out, and

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:22.025
actually realized that I wanted to try
more traditional early-stage investing,

00:03:22.025 --> 00:03:23.350
after that.

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:27.930
Wasn't really sure how impact investing
was defined, at least in my mind.

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:32.380
So, through a summer internship,
I tried traditional venture capital.

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:33.160
Really enjoyed that.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:36.500
Will likely also be returning
to my summer internship.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:40.170
And, would say, I think resources that
were helpful along the way, for me,

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:44.870
also took advantage of our alum network in
terms of just learning about what they do.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:51.090
Helping recruiting and then our career
management office was really great too.

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:54.590
Had a lot of one-on-one sessions
with our career advisors and

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:59.370
they really helped me hone in on what I
was interested in and what I wanted to do.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:01.570
&gt;&gt; Amazing, that sounds great.

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:05.080
We also have a question
about global experiences.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:07.860
I just talked earlier
about the requirement,

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:11.160
as well as the portfolio of
opportunities that are available.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:16.119
Could each of you share kind of what your
global experiences look like and what the.

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:20.910
Experience was like for you.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:25.870
&gt;&gt; Sure, so last year I went on two
of the trips that the GSTs that

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:27.360
the Global Study Trips.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:30.190
My first one was in
the winter to Thailand.

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:32.840
And then my second was to South Africa.

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:35.962
And I would say both of
them were incredible.

00:04:35.962 --> 00:04:39.120
I hadn't been to either
of those countries and

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.093
the trips really help you
learn about the country.

00:04:42.093 --> 00:04:45.690
There's a big cultural component
that goes into just learning about

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:47.287
some the foods and some of the customs.

00:04:47.287 --> 00:04:50.490
You have to do meetings beforehand
which were really helpful

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:54.830
in hitting the ground and knowing what to
do and not feeling like a total outsider.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:58.130
And I also loved getting to know
these very different economies.

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:02.700
And there's a sovereign nation in
Thailand that we had to think about,

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:06.940
learning about agriculture there
was really fascinating and,

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:08.940
those were probably two of
my favorite experiences.

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:10.840
Is at the GSB in the first year.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:11.940
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:15.010
&gt;&gt; And I did a trip over spring
break to go to Brazil and it was

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:18.580
specifically a social innovation trip
focused on health and education in Brazil.

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:22.590
So likewise to Sara, there was just so
much that you could learn there.

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:26.480
What I really like about it was we met
from everyone, from the former President

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:30.930
of Brazil during a financial crisis
that was going on in the country,

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:34.960
all the way through to a front line
clinic, in the northeast of the country.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:38.940
So, kind of getting in and everything in
between, so a wide breadth of experiences.

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:42.500
The other thing I would add is it such
an amazing opportunity to really, or

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:46.030
another amazing opportunity, to really
get to know your classmates well.

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:50.020
Twenty people are on the trip and
you're spending ten days with them and

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:51.765
a lot time each of those days.

00:05:51.765 --> 00:05:54.600
And so it's another great opportunity
to to build relationships within

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:55.600
the community here.

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:56.100
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:01.560
So pivoting a little bit
to the campus experience,

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:06.340
did either of you have a significant
other and can you talk

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:09.560
a little bit about the experience of
coming to the GSB with a partner.

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:13.440
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:18.470
My experiences might be
a little bit different,

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:24.370
my boyfriend is also a student in Nick and
my class, also an MBA 2 currently,

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:31.400
and so it's the first time that we were
in school together, I would definitely

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:36.420
say it's tough because probably as
everyone's heard, it can get really busy.

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:38.970
So it's important to set
aside time with one another.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:43.520
But I think for us, we really viewed,
and I think this is true for

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:46.830
every couples, where one person is
a student and one person is not.

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:49.630
It's really a mutual growing
experience and there are so

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:53.620
many opportunities for
your partner to be involved or

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:57.130
not involved depending on what
kind of path you decide to carve.

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:59.690
And it can be a huge
learning experience for

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:02.840
both of you whether you're
both students or not.

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:04.770
&gt;&gt; And so I don't have an SO
while being here but

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:08.060
I will add there are a lot of other
resources that are available.

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:12.660
There are actually clubs for SOs and
you get integrated within the campus.

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:13.440
So, your SO

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:17.120
actually gets a card to go to the gym and
the sporting events that are here.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:22.320
And then even some of the most
popular classes within the GSB your SO

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:23.920
can take within their own section.

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:27.150
So, for example, touchy feely is
a really well known course here and

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:28.820
they have special sections for SOs.

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:31.830
So I think there's a lot of effort
to culturally integrate them

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:32.900
in to the community.

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:34.770
So it's kind of one large class.

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:38.870
&gt;&gt; We also have a question
about financial aid and

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:40.740
is it offered to international students?

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:43.234
So I can actually take that one because
I don't think either one of you

00:07:43.234 --> 00:07:44.357
are international students.

00:07:44.357 --> 00:07:49.626
But we do offer need-based financial aid
for every single person who's admitted

00:07:49.626 --> 00:07:54.667
to our program and we don't differentiate
between international students or

00:07:54.667 --> 00:07:58.276
American students in terms
of access to financial aid.

00:07:58.276 --> 00:08:03.727
But maybe each of you could speak a little
bit of the cost benefit Thoughts that went

00:08:03.727 --> 00:08:08.941
through your mind when you were pursuing
an MBA and ways that you financed it and

00:08:08.941 --> 00:08:13.130
thought about investing in your
education here at Standford.

00:08:15.335 --> 00:08:18.795
So, I came from a background of
having done four years of living and

00:08:18.795 --> 00:08:22.325
working in sub Saharan Africa, so I was
a recipient of some of the need based

00:08:22.325 --> 00:08:26.605
financial aid, which very much made
that a positive MPV calculation for me.

00:08:28.365 --> 00:08:33.565
It's amazing about how much recruiters
start coming to you, as a GSP student,

00:08:33.565 --> 00:08:35.495
all the way through on campus recruiting,

00:08:35.495 --> 00:08:38.175
to just random kind of cold emails
that happen during your second year.

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:43.560
So, the bump in salary could
be pretty significant,

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:47.040
and more importantly maybe the salary but

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:51.553
the actual opportunities
that you can do while in

00:08:51.553 --> 00:08:56.720
some of these organizations makes it MPV
positive for me within one or two years.

00:08:56.720 --> 00:08:59.430
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think for me I didn't receive a
substantial fellowship, actually very low,

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:02.320
and have been relying primarily on loans.

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:05.840
And so I think the cost benefit for
me is actually still,

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:08.200
the benefit is harder to measure,
at least in the near term.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:15.200
But for me, the GSB has opened up doors
as Nick mentioned, that I would never

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:18.190
have been able to walk through before
coming, or even known that I wanted to.

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:20.130
And I think before coming in,

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:23.830
there was honestly a question
in my mind of This is expensive.

00:09:23.830 --> 00:09:28.870
You know, consulting is also general,
you can go to something else.

00:09:28.870 --> 00:09:30.530
And I think having this
hindsight by us now,

00:09:30.530 --> 00:09:33.270
I'm really glad that I
did make that tradeoff.

00:09:33.270 --> 00:09:36.440
And I just want to say too,
if you're not a fellowship recipient,

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:39.570
the school and
also their private resources are.

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:40.140
That are.

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:43.975
Both are really good in
helping you bridge that gap.

00:09:43.975 --> 00:09:47.529
And it's what I rely on for my education.

00:09:47.529 --> 00:09:49.928
&gt;&gt; Great.
So, aside from great weather,

00:09:49.928 --> 00:09:55.250
someone is asking us what are some
of the benefits of our location?

00:09:55.250 --> 00:09:58.220
Where we are either geopolitically or
in terms of the valley.

00:09:58.220 --> 00:10:01.830
Have you taken advantage of local
resources that are around campus?

00:10:05.590 --> 00:10:07.550
&gt;&gt; From a lifestyle perspective,
I love hiking.

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:09.820
And if you haven't had a chance
to come to the Bay and

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:13.240
figure out what redwoods are,
they're these massive trees.

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:16.720
Some of the most beautiful
hiking I think in the world,

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:18.870
in terms of day hikes
are right around here.

00:10:18.870 --> 00:10:22.900
San Francisco is an amazing city,
just culturally, it's great.

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:25.180
Silicon Valley.

00:10:25.180 --> 00:10:29.170
So, I think the benefit of the school
is we get a lot of entrepreneurs and

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:32.220
venture capitalists that
come in to speak and

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:35.260
teach classes here that you probably
wouldn't get outside of the valley.

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:37.935
Just because of
the resources that are here.

00:10:37.935 --> 00:10:40.050
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; I don't know if you have.

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:42.810
&gt;&gt; Yeah I would say Nick
pretty much covered it and

00:10:42.810 --> 00:10:48.340
just add that for a lot of industries like
venture capital which I'm interested in.

00:10:48.340 --> 00:10:49.830
Also tech but many more.

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:52.950
Healthcare, consumer,
there are so many interesting and

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:55.340
exciting companies within
an hour drive of us.

00:10:55.340 --> 00:10:59.990
And so, I think getting to visit them,
whether that's through interviews or

00:10:59.990 --> 00:11:01.820
just learning, has been really exciting.

00:11:01.820 --> 00:11:03.960
And you're kind of in the middle of it, so

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:08.050
I would say something less tangible is
just the energy that you get from that.

00:11:08.050 --> 00:11:11.720
And you know I grew up in Chicago, went
to school in Boston, lived in New York.

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:15.090
I do think it's pretty
unique to this area.

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:19.165
&gt;&gt; Skiing and surfing is also readily
accessible if you're into those types of

00:11:19.165 --> 00:11:19.923
[CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; And

00:11:19.923 --> 00:11:22.140
on the same day if you
are really ambitious.

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:23.784
[CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, you can ski in the morning and

00:11:23.784 --> 00:11:24.920
surf in the afternoon.

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:25.427
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; So,

00:11:25.427 --> 00:11:30.500
along the same lines having access
to all of these companies, do

00:11:30.500 --> 00:11:34.250
you have any concrete examples you really
wanted to explore in patent busting?

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:39.020
And we have a question about access
to social entrepreneurship and

00:11:39.020 --> 00:11:43.480
having been previously involved
in social impact related work.

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:47.336
Do you have any thoughts on resources for
that in particular?

00:11:47.336 --> 00:11:51.548
Either within

00:11:51.548 --> 00:11:57.830
the region or us in the Stanford MBA
Program, which want to touch briefly.

00:11:57.830 --> 00:11:58.540
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
So, I think you know,

00:11:58.540 --> 00:12:00.020
I briefly mentioned this before.

00:12:00.020 --> 00:12:02.510
But I think the impact,
both impact investing and

00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:06.200
then also just impact labs
program is a program offered by

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:08.230
the GSB where your place,
either with the non-profit or

00:12:08.230 --> 00:12:12.250
an impact investment fund and you get
the intern for them for school credit.

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:14.980
I would also say more generally
that social innovation

00:12:14.980 --> 00:12:17.910
is very very well resourced at the GSB and

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:22.660
at Stanford broadly so
there's a social innovation club.

00:12:22.660 --> 00:12:25.940
A student club but there are also
resources at the center that you

00:12:27.490 --> 00:12:33.630
can take advantage of to test
different Hypotheses that you want to.

00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:34.780
&gt;&gt; So, just to add on that the Center for

00:12:34.780 --> 00:12:38.240
Social Innovation actually
has GSB staff on that.

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:39.950
They have trips so

00:12:39.950 --> 00:12:43.700
GST global study trips that will focus
specifically on social innovation.

00:12:43.700 --> 00:12:48.220
I was on one in Brazil and
then there's another program called SEED

00:12:48.220 --> 00:12:50.120
which has an acronym I don't
know what it stands for.

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:54.360
But essentially is money that is set aside
to invest with social entrepreneurs.

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:55.240
And that.

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:58.380
That materializes a few ways
from a student experience.

00:12:58.380 --> 00:13:01.440
So, one is a lot of times those
social entrepreneurs from around

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:05.410
the world will be here on campus and
you have access to them.

00:13:05.410 --> 00:13:08.460
And then secondly to that,
there's a chance to take

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:12.870
funding to work in social entrepreneurship
as part of your internship experience.

00:13:12.870 --> 00:13:15.330
So, if a organization can qualify

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:19.660
through Stanford to accept funding It
allows you to get closer to market rate as

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:22.510
a student to work in social
entrepreneurship over the summer.

00:13:22.510 --> 00:13:26.370
And then also they have a loan forgiveness
program to certain students should you

00:13:26.370 --> 00:13:29.620
launch a career with a non-profit
in social entrepreneur afterwards.

00:13:29.620 --> 00:13:30.640
So, I think there are lots of access and

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:34.850
lots of resources, and I'd argue one
of the core competencies of the school.

00:13:34.850 --> 00:13:36.520
Great those are really
helpful concrete examples.

00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:41.700
So, Tom has a question for you guys about
aspects of your experience where you may

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:45.060
have been challenged beyond
what you expected at the GSB.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:48.240
And what is the most important

00:13:49.270 --> 00:13:54.255
piece of advice that you would
give to your first year self?

00:13:54.255 --> 00:13:57.641
Looking back now.

00:14:01.123 --> 00:14:02.710
&gt;&gt; So, yeah.

00:14:02.710 --> 00:14:05.560
So, I think the first thing is be open and
ready to change.

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:09.620
So, one of the great
parts of the curriculum

00:14:09.620 --> 00:14:13.360
here have to do with interpersonal
skills and leadership development.

00:14:13.360 --> 00:14:15.260
There are so many mental models or
there were so

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:19.820
many mental models that I held coming
into the GSB about what leadership is.

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:23.440
How people react in different situations
and being in such a diverse environment

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:27.930
and having a very open culture of feedback
and the way the curriculum is set up.

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:30.440
You really get to challenge
those mental models.

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:33.930
And I think some of the most challenging
part were in a team situation or

00:14:33.930 --> 00:14:39.530
in some of these courses where you kind
of really get down to having to be

00:14:39.530 --> 00:14:43.230
vulnerable, to get to a point to change
and become a much more effective leader.

00:14:43.230 --> 00:14:45.130
So, being open to that process is really,

00:14:45.130 --> 00:14:49.540
really important and can sometimes take
a few weeks to get comfortable in.

00:14:49.540 --> 00:14:50.930
&gt;&gt; Hm.

00:14:50.930 --> 00:14:54.020
Yeah, I would say,
sort of along this same theme,

00:14:54.020 --> 00:14:58.790
before the JSV I wouldn't
consider myself a leader.

00:14:58.790 --> 00:15:01.750
I was very good at getting the job
that was asked of me done and

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:06.500
always at a junior level as an analyst or
associate And, at the GSV, it's

00:15:06.500 --> 00:15:10.870
the first time I've ever, in my life, felt
like I have the confidence to be a leader.

00:15:10.870 --> 00:15:14.930
And, I think that's actually been
associated with the things that

00:15:14.930 --> 00:15:16.330
have been challenging at the GSV.

00:15:16.330 --> 00:15:20.050
So, I'm one of the co-presidents
of Women In Management, this year.

00:15:20.050 --> 00:15:25.170
And, I help lead a board of 10 people,
we've planned retreats for

00:15:25.170 --> 00:15:29.650
the full class and
just thinking about things like how do you

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:35.490
create a board culture, how do you
motivate people who are volunteers and

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:37.410
your friends but
need to get a lot of work done.

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:40.680
Among an already busy schedule and I think
those are real things that managers and

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:42.242
leaders have to face.

00:15:42.242 --> 00:15:45.860
I've never gotten
the opportunity to do so, and

00:15:45.860 --> 00:15:49.224
I just learned so
much from that experience.

00:15:49.224 --> 00:15:53.808
I think my advice to my first year self or
any first years would be, be open like

00:15:53.808 --> 00:15:58.267
Nick said, but also just take the chance
and have confidence in yourself.

00:15:58.267 --> 00:16:02.655
I think having confidence sometimes
Just comes from doing it anyway even if

00:16:02.655 --> 00:16:04.266
you think you can't do it.

00:16:04.266 --> 00:16:07.290
And asking yourself why not you.

00:16:07.290 --> 00:16:11.340
And I think the GSB has made me ask
myself that over and over again.

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:14.640
And I've just taken on way more than
I would have thought I could have.

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:18.679
&gt;&gt; That's not only great advice for first
year but probably great advice for life.

00:16:18.679 --> 00:16:19.255
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:16:19.255 --> 00:16:22.330
I think I'll probably
take that one with me.

00:16:22.330 --> 00:16:26.620
So you touched a little bit about
the challenges of being a club leader.

00:16:26.620 --> 00:16:29.640
Balancing that with a lot of
other obligations at school.

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:32.350
So we've got a question
about a typical week.

00:16:32.350 --> 00:16:37.027
How are you balancing cut and
dry classwork, leadership opportunities

00:16:37.027 --> 00:16:41.480
through clubs, other initiatives
that you might be involved with?

00:16:41.480 --> 00:16:44.718
How does that break down and
how do you end up prioritizing your time?

00:16:48.486 --> 00:16:53.051
&gt;&gt; Sure, so I guess I'll preface this
with I was given the advice, and

00:16:53.051 --> 00:16:56.415
I haven't followed as
closely as I should have but

00:16:56.415 --> 00:17:00.423
it's definitely good advice
to ruthlessly prioritize and

00:17:00.423 --> 00:17:05.330
do that by coming in, and
obviously being adaptive, and flexible.

00:17:05.330 --> 00:17:08.770
But coming in with a clear list
of what your priorities are,

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:12.840
whether that's academics,
or social, job related.

00:17:12.840 --> 00:17:16.780
Being pretty specific about them,
just coming in,

00:17:16.780 --> 00:17:19.250
because I think a big
struggle at least for

00:17:19.250 --> 00:17:23.490
me at the GSB is not letting the urgent
get in the way of the important.

00:17:23.490 --> 00:17:27.190
So I would say the topic of time
management has been tough for

00:17:27.190 --> 00:17:29.970
me because there are so
many interesting things to do here.

00:17:29.970 --> 00:17:34.213
In terms of a typical week, and
this kind of ties back to my priorities,

00:17:34.213 --> 00:17:37.562
which I think number one
we're around meeting people.

00:17:37.562 --> 00:17:42.190
That's one of the big reasons I came here,
just to get to experience the people here.

00:17:42.190 --> 00:17:46.457
And then I would say for me,
I might be in a minority here, but

00:17:46.457 --> 00:17:49.240
academics are really important to me.

00:17:49.240 --> 00:17:53.580
And I've loved the classes that I've been
in and the professors that I've had, so

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:56.070
I would say I spend a fair
amount of time on academics.

00:17:56.070 --> 00:18:00.931
And then I mentioned Women Management and
a few other extracurriculars that I'm in.

00:18:00.931 --> 00:18:03.763
I would say I pretty much spread
my time among those three.

00:18:03.763 --> 00:18:06.811
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; And right now, for me at least,

00:18:06.811 --> 00:18:11.648
the extracurriculars are taking
a bigger role in my life.

00:18:11.648 --> 00:18:17.930
I think through prioritization, I try
to go back to my list that I created.

00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:22.353
And update every now and then to say,
I said that this is more important to me.

00:18:22.353 --> 00:18:26.324
Let me not cancel on a meeting
with a friend because of this

00:18:26.324 --> 00:18:29.845
particular thing which I
know is less important.

00:18:29.845 --> 00:18:31.670
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:18:31.670 --> 00:18:33.570
&gt;&gt; And yeah.
I'm trying to think about also how that

00:18:33.570 --> 00:18:35.840
changed my first year to my second year.

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:39.100
So first year, I think academics
probably require a little bit more work

00:18:39.100 --> 00:18:41.560
outside of the classroom than
they do your second year.

00:18:41.560 --> 00:18:45.100
In your second year you
have all electives.

00:18:45.100 --> 00:18:49.155
So also because you've chosen every single
class you want to be in, working for

00:18:49.155 --> 00:18:51.100
them doesn't really feel like work.

00:18:51.100 --> 00:18:56.398
It's intellectually
stimulating on all levels.

00:18:56.398 --> 00:18:59.940
And so I probably, I don't know,
could give an hour break down, but

00:18:59.940 --> 00:19:03.791
there is a decent amount of time for
class prep meeting other classmates and

00:19:03.791 --> 00:19:08.131
building relationships with classmates did
take a significant amount of time, but

00:19:08.131 --> 00:19:10.300
I mean, that's something I love doing.

00:19:10.300 --> 00:19:12.890
I also need a lot of personal time for
myself.

00:19:12.890 --> 00:19:17.220
So I block out at least
an hour a day just to,

00:19:17.220 --> 00:19:21.840
that's on my calendar that no
one else can schedule over.

00:19:21.840 --> 00:19:25.000
There's a great way to balance academics,

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:28.920
recruiting takes up a lot of time
depending which cycle you go through.

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:31.700
And then yeah, just prioritize at what

00:19:31.700 --> 00:19:34.030
quarter which of those are most
important for you to go through.

00:19:35.720 --> 00:19:38.750
&gt;&gt; Have either of you thought about
pursuing a joint or dual degree?

00:19:41.880 --> 00:19:43.800
&gt;&gt; I have not.
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:45.770
&gt;&gt; I thought about doing
the Masters of Education.

00:19:45.770 --> 00:19:47.478
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; And I decided against it.

00:19:47.478 --> 00:19:49.400
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:19:49.400 --> 00:19:51.150
I mean, and
the reason I wanted to do it was so

00:19:51.150 --> 00:19:53.860
that I could get an extra quarter so
I could get two internships.

00:19:53.860 --> 00:19:56.570
And that really just kind of
came back to prioritizing,

00:19:56.570 --> 00:19:59.400
because I was kind of thinking,
did I want to do health or education?

00:19:59.400 --> 00:20:03.707
And just did kind of a cost benefit
analysis and decided against it.

00:20:03.707 --> 00:20:04.304
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; But

00:20:04.304 --> 00:20:07.872
we have obviously lots of
classmates that are doing JD/MBAs,

00:20:07.872 --> 00:20:11.254
that are doing MD/MBAs, and
they seem to really enjoy it.

00:20:11.254 --> 00:20:13.735
&gt;&gt; Yeah and
it an important personal calculus to make.

00:20:13.735 --> 00:20:15.455
Just like everything else with a GSB.

00:20:15.455 --> 00:20:17.175
It's kind of a choose your own adventure,
and

00:20:17.175 --> 00:20:21.075
you always need to think through the trade
offs of what do I gain from this, and

00:20:21.075 --> 00:20:23.945
what are the advantages of doing it or
not doing it?

00:20:23.945 --> 00:20:27.828
Did you dip your toes into the water of
maybe taking classes at the add school or

00:20:27.828 --> 00:20:30.277
have either of you taken classes,
what we call,

00:20:30.277 --> 00:20:33.465
across the street at any of
the other grad schools at Stanford?

00:20:35.837 --> 00:20:40.392
&gt;&gt; I have not yet, I'm hoping next
quarter to take what's called bio-design,

00:20:40.392 --> 00:20:45.223
which I think is actually a med school
class, but it's cross-functional between

00:20:45.223 --> 00:20:49.729
engineering, medicine, and business
to launch a medical device product.

00:20:49.729 --> 00:20:50.935
&gt;&gt; Interesting.

00:20:50.935 --> 00:20:53.200
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would say I'm in the same camp.

00:20:53.200 --> 00:20:55.951
I've just found there to be so
many interesting GSP classes.

00:20:55.951 --> 00:20:58.760
That being said though,
something that I came in really

00:20:58.760 --> 00:21:01.509
wanting to get a better grasp
of is computer science and

00:21:01.509 --> 00:21:05.474
obviously Stanford is one of the best
computer science programs in the world.

00:21:05.474 --> 00:21:10.071
And so for next quarter I am signed up for
a computer science class and I think,

00:21:10.071 --> 00:21:14.740
something that I would also like to try,
it's just more with a design school,

00:21:14.740 --> 00:21:19.510
which I think has that very unique type
of thinking that we can all benefit from.

00:21:19.510 --> 00:21:20.460
&gt;&gt; Great.
&gt;&gt; I'm also taking

00:21:20.460 --> 00:21:22.540
rock climbing next quarter,
I forgot to throw that in there.

00:21:22.540 --> 00:21:26.715
So there are lots of PE classes that are
available [LAUGH] everything from hip hop,

00:21:26.715 --> 00:21:29.105
to rock climbing,
to horseback riding, to golf.

00:21:29.105 --> 00:21:32.231
Golf is a very, very popular
across this [INAUDIBLE] elective.

00:21:32.231 --> 00:21:34.255
&gt;&gt; It is.
&gt;&gt; It's sunny all the time here.

00:21:34.255 --> 00:21:36.037
&gt;&gt; Yeah it's a beautiful driving range.

00:21:36.037 --> 00:21:40.135
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I've gotten a few questions for

00:21:40.135 --> 00:21:42.945
admissions so I'm just going to
address those really quickly.

00:21:42.945 --> 00:21:47.530
One was for
students who apply from college directly.

00:21:47.530 --> 00:21:49.953
So if you're currently
a senior in college,

00:21:49.953 --> 00:21:51.875
you are more than welcome to apply.

00:21:51.875 --> 00:21:55.908
You go through the exact same application
process and you, actually we're looking

00:21:55.908 --> 00:21:59.680
for the same three criteria that we
talked about earlier in the presentation.

00:21:59.680 --> 00:22:01.980
So, we're not looking for
anything different.

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:04.100
If you're thinking about
letters of recommendation,

00:22:04.100 --> 00:22:07.210
you might want to think about
your summer internships.

00:22:07.210 --> 00:22:11.350
Faculty members tend not to be
the best recommenders in our view.

00:22:11.350 --> 00:22:14.580
We're looking for
people who really can highlight your

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:17.110
professional competencies
as well as your leadership.

00:22:17.110 --> 00:22:21.860
So if a professor was an adviser to
you for a club, for example, or for

00:22:21.860 --> 00:22:24.360
your research,
that would be really helpful.

00:22:24.360 --> 00:22:28.190
But if it was just a student-teacher
relationship, typically that's where I see

00:22:28.190 --> 00:22:34.230
college seniors benefiting more from
using your internship supervisors,

00:22:34.230 --> 00:22:37.880
or other people that you've kind
of been held accountable to.

00:22:37.880 --> 00:22:42.800
Once you get in, we typically ask
you to defer your enrollment,

00:22:42.800 --> 00:22:47.170
so you actually go and get some work
experience for a couple years, and

00:22:47.170 --> 00:22:51.780
then matriculate into the GSP
after those two years.

00:22:52.820 --> 00:22:55.959
In terms of making yourself
competitive as an undergrad,

00:22:55.959 --> 00:22:59.417
the same advice actually goes
to everybody, which is you know,

00:22:59.417 --> 00:23:03.595
we are looking for people that demonstrate
a lot of intellectual curiosity.

00:23:03.595 --> 00:23:08.065
So take tough classes,
challenge yourself, perform well in them.

00:23:08.065 --> 00:23:10.678
Get involved in activities
in your community and

00:23:10.678 --> 00:23:12.981
demonstrate your leadership potential.

00:23:12.981 --> 00:23:16.355
Those are things we're looking for
across all our candidates.

00:23:16.355 --> 00:23:19.215
Similarly for joint and
dual degree programs we're not looking for

00:23:19.215 --> 00:23:21.225
any separate admissions criteria.

00:23:21.225 --> 00:23:23.805
We're looking for
the same three things for our program.

00:23:23.805 --> 00:23:27.173
However, you will also have to meet
the admissions requirement for

00:23:27.173 --> 00:23:28.366
those other programs.

00:23:28.366 --> 00:23:33.077
So, whatever the requirements are,
say for law school or for medical school,

00:23:33.077 --> 00:23:37.156
those are not things that we would
be looking for in our candidates but

00:23:37.156 --> 00:23:41.895
they are things that you would obviously
have to meet for those other programs.

00:23:41.895 --> 00:23:46.095
We also had a question about placing
weight on every component of

00:23:46.095 --> 00:23:47.820
the application itself.

00:23:47.820 --> 00:23:51.145
For example,
do we weigh the GPA versus tests,

00:23:51.145 --> 00:23:54.483
versus recommendation letters differently.

00:23:54.483 --> 00:23:58.101
And I wish I could tell you that
there were some secret formula for

00:23:58.101 --> 00:24:00.317
how to read an application and create.

00:24:00.317 --> 00:24:03.905
A calculus for it,
it would make my job way easier, but

00:24:03.905 --> 00:24:06.330
actually it's an art and a science.

00:24:06.330 --> 00:24:10.673
So we don't have any sort
of equation that we use, so

00:24:10.673 --> 00:24:15.830
by definition there is no weight
placed on any one component.

00:24:15.830 --> 00:24:20.790
We talked a little bit earlier about how
we have a holistic application process.

00:24:20.790 --> 00:24:22.590
And what that means, fundamentally,

00:24:22.590 --> 00:24:25.960
is that we look at every single
piece of your application, so

00:24:25.960 --> 00:24:32.110
every single thing counts, and everything
is a part of our review process.

00:24:32.110 --> 00:24:36.060
We try to weave all three criteria
of intellect, leadership, and

00:24:36.060 --> 00:24:40.090
personal qualities into our
evaluation of you as a candidate.

00:24:40.090 --> 00:24:46.420
And we really pour a lot of time and
energy and appreciation for

00:24:46.420 --> 00:24:50.350
how much you invest in your application
by taking a holistic approach to it.

00:24:50.350 --> 00:24:53.780
So there's no one piece of your
application that kind of makes or

00:24:53.780 --> 00:24:54.780
breaks it.

00:24:54.780 --> 00:24:58.830
Even if you're invited to interview, it's
not like the interview tips you in or out.

00:24:58.830 --> 00:25:02.560
That goes back in to your application,
which is re-evaluated as a whole.

00:25:02.560 --> 00:25:07.196
So every single piece matters, and
not one piece matters more than the other.

00:25:07.196 --> 00:25:09.881
So we're coming up on
the last five minutes, and

00:25:09.881 --> 00:25:13.744
we had a student ask a question that
I think would be, or not a student,

00:25:13.744 --> 00:25:17.497
a participant, ask a question that
could be a good capstone for us.

00:25:17.497 --> 00:25:23.077
And that is have you had one monumental
experience so far at the GSB?

00:25:23.077 --> 00:25:26.487
And I could phrase it another way,
which would be,

00:25:26.487 --> 00:25:29.670
can you talk about your favorite memory so
far?

00:25:29.670 --> 00:25:34.303
And it can be anything, it doesn't have to
academic, or if it is that's great, but

00:25:34.303 --> 00:25:37.636
anything about your experience
that you would say stands out

00:25:37.636 --> 00:25:39.298
as your favorite part so far?

00:25:43.756 --> 00:25:44.800
&gt;&gt; Ooh, tough question.

00:25:44.800 --> 00:25:48.091
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You can only pick one.

00:25:48.091 --> 00:25:49.180
&gt;&gt; Only one.
[LAUGH]

00:25:49.180 --> 00:25:51.856
&gt;&gt; Actually I have one, I have mine.

00:25:51.856 --> 00:25:55.316
I mentioned that I am co-leading
Women in Management this year.

00:25:55.316 --> 00:25:58.265
It's been a really challenging experience.

00:25:58.265 --> 00:25:59.935
Part of the reason that it was so

00:25:59.935 --> 00:26:02.769
challenging was planning
our annual fall retreat.

00:26:02.769 --> 00:26:10.059
160 women come together for a weekend,
and really it's ours to plan.

00:26:10.059 --> 00:26:14.225
We have last year's curriculum, but
we decided to go rogue this year and

00:26:14.225 --> 00:26:15.400
do it from scratch.

00:26:15.400 --> 00:26:20.720
And it was incredibly stressful,
the months leading up to it.

00:26:20.720 --> 00:26:25.650
And I would say it was
the most fulfilling moment for

00:26:25.650 --> 00:26:30.630
me at GSB because of what happened that
weekend, and also just the, I would

00:26:32.030 --> 00:26:35.650
say, the discussions, but really the sense
of community that came out of that.

00:26:35.650 --> 00:26:39.023
I think when you get to the GSB,
especially your first year,

00:26:39.023 --> 00:26:42.939
it's just kind of crazy, in good ways,
but also in not so great ways.

00:26:42.939 --> 00:26:48.231
And the opportunity to provide a retreat
where particularly first year MBAs

00:26:48.231 --> 00:26:53.520
could hit the reset button and say,
hey, let's breathe, let's pause and

00:26:53.520 --> 00:26:58.564
let's remember why we came here, and
also talk about what's holding us

00:26:58.564 --> 00:27:03.711
back from being our fullest selves,
and really be authentic and honest.

00:27:03.711 --> 00:27:07.035
I got to facilitate those sessions for
the first time, and just

00:27:07.035 --> 00:27:11.374
hearing those discussions come out of that
made all of the effort really worth it.

00:27:11.374 --> 00:27:13.731
And I think just the community element to,
interclass,

00:27:13.731 --> 00:27:16.270
which I wanted more of last year,
was really special for me.

00:27:16.270 --> 00:27:21.660
&gt;&gt; I think there had been a lot of
profound or impactful moments that have

00:27:21.660 --> 00:27:24.460
happened within a lot of
the interpersonal dynamic classes and

00:27:24.460 --> 00:27:29.130
leadership classes that I've had, but
those are going to take awhile to set up.

00:27:29.130 --> 00:27:34.000
So I'd say probably one of the more
pinch myself moments was I

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:38.220
was in a class with Dr. Condoleezza Rice,
who was the former US Secretary of State.

00:27:38.220 --> 00:27:44.110
And we had a case simulation that she was
running about what to do from a business

00:27:44.110 --> 00:27:48.780
standpoint in eastern Europe, assuming
this fictitious country invaded this,

00:27:48.780 --> 00:27:53.130
and you had resources in that country,
and ended up getting in to like, a very,

00:27:53.130 --> 00:27:57.503
very small pseudo debate,
about the trip wire theory of NATO.

00:27:57.503 --> 00:27:58.783
&gt;&gt; Wow.
[LAUGH]

00:27:58.783 --> 00:28:01.720
&gt;&gt; Which was crazy that this is one

00:28:01.720 --> 00:28:05.410
